        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "fe511d3a-f28c-5cd5-88cd-7c8f81ce9d90",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: California, southeastern California, Yuma, Arizona, Colorado River, Calexico, Imperial Valley, San Joaquin River\n- Products: Madera Canal, All-American Canal, Delta–Mendota Canal, Friant-Kern Canal\n- Systems: Central Valley Project\n- Structures: Imperial Dam, Friant Dam\n\nFacts:\n- The Madera Canal is a 35.9-mile aqueduct in California.\n- The All-American Canal is an 80-mile aqueduct in southeastern California.\n- Imperial Dam on the Colorado River, about 30 miles northeast of Yuma, Arizona, diverts water into the All-American Canal.\n- The All-American Canal runs to just west of Calexico, California, before its last branch heads mostly north into the Imperial Valley.\n- The Delta–Mendota Canal is part of the Central Valley Project.\n- The purpose of the Delta–Mendota Canal is to replace water in the San Joaquin River that is diverted at Friant Dam into the Madera Canal and the Friant-Kern Canal.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
